import java.awt.Color;
import java.awt.Graphics2D;

public class FadeIn {
    private Color color = new Color(0, 0, 0); // Black initially

    public void setAlpha(int alpha) {
        this.color = new Color(this.color.getRed(), this.color.getGreen(), this.color.getBlue(), alpha);
    }

    public void draw(Graphics2D g2d) {
        g2d.setColor(color);
        g2d.fillRect(0, 0, 100, 50); // Draw a rectangle with the specified color and size
    }
}
```

This class can be used to control the transparency (alpha value) of a graphical element, allowing it to gradually become visible over time. The `setAlpha` method allows setting the final alpha level, and the `draw` method uses that alpha level to render the element.

To use this class, you would typically call `setAlpha` before drawing elements that should fade in, like text or images. For instance:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Graphics2D g2d = ...; // Get a graphics context
    FadeIn fadeInEffect = new FadeIn();

    // Set the initial alpha level
    fadeInEffect.setAlpha(0);

    // Draw something that will fade in
    fadeInEffect.draw(g2d);

    // Gradually increase the alpha level
    for (int i = 0; i <= 255; i++) {
        fadeInEffect.setAlpha(i);
        fadeInEffect.draw(g2d);

        try {
            Thread.sleep(10); // Sleep for 10 milliseconds between each update
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

